The '''1996 United States Senate special election in Oregon''' was held on January 30, 1996, to fill the seat vacated by Republican [[Bob Packwood]], who had resigned from the Senate due to sexual misconduct allegations. Governor at the time [[John Kitzhaber]] was not permitted by state law to appoint anyone to temporarily fill in for the rest of Packwood's term and called for a special election on January 30, 1996.

In the primaries held on December 5, 1995, Democratic [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. Representative]] [[Ron Wyden]] and Republican Oregon State Senate [[List of Presidents of the Oregon State Senate|President]] [[Gordon H. Smith]] were nominated. Wyden then defeated Smith in the general election.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=3468|title = Our Campaigns - OR US Senate - Special Election Race - Jan 30, 1996}}</ref> Smith won the [[1996 United States Senate election in Oregon|regularly-scheduled election]] to Oregon’s other Senate seat later that year and served alongside Wyden until 2009. Wyden's victory made him the first Democratic senator from Oregon since 1969, when [[Wayne Morse]] left office after narrowly losing re-election to Packwood.

